---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

// A document rather than a marketing page: narrower measure, numbered
// sections, and a print stylesheet so it sets as two pages on paper.
//
// The company around it is invented, but every technical claim here is true
// of the real scripts at github.com/erinlkolp/computercraft-scripts. Check
// against the source before editing any number or guarantee below.
const REPO = 'https://github.com/erinlkolp/computercraft-scripts';

// Appendix figures. Every value is the shipped default read out of the real
// scripts, and `ident` is the actual constant name that carries it -- which is
// the point of printing the column. Re-read the source before changing any of
// these; CI cannot catch drift here.
const specs = [
  {
    id: 'a1',
    heading: 'A.1  Extraction unit',
    source: 'turtles/flattener.lua',
    rows: [
      ['Working area', '15 \u00d7 15 cells', 'WIDTH \u00d7 LENGTH'],
      ['Vertical ceiling', '32 layers above the floor', 'MAX_HEIGHT'],
      ['Unconditional sweep depth', '4 layers', 'SCAN_MIN'],
      ['Re-cuts per block', '8', 'DIG_RETRY'],
      ['Move attempts before a cell is abandoned', '6', 'MAX_STUCK'],
      ['Refuelling threshold', '200 blocks of travel', 'FUEL_MIN'],
      ['Reserve held over the trip home', '16 blocks of travel', 'FUEL_MARGIN'],
      ['Onboard capacity', '16 slots', '\u2014'],
      ['Accepted fuel', 'Coal, charcoal, coal block, blaze rod', 'FUEL_ITEMS'],
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'a2',
    heading: 'A.2  Collection unit',
    source: 'turtles/sweeper.lua',
    rows: [
      ['Working area', '10 \u00d7 10 cells', 'WIDTH \u00d7 LENGTH'],
      ['Cruise altitude', '1 block above the litter layer', 'SWEEP_ALT'],
      ['Interval between passes', '3,600 s (one hour)', 'PATROL_DELAY'],
      ['Recovery attempts per cell', '64', 'SUCK_LIMIT'],
      ['Climb allowance', '3 blocks', 'CLIMB_LIMIT'],
      ['Move attempts before a cell is abandoned', '3', 'MAX_STUCK'],
      ['Refuelling threshold', '200 blocks of travel', 'FUEL_MIN'],
      ['Reserve held over the trip home', '16 blocks of travel', 'FUEL_MARGIN'],
    ],
  },
];

// Verbatim test names from test/flattener_test.lua.
const testNames = [
  'never aims a dig below the start layer',
  'leaves blocks outside the 15x15 alone',
  'does not mine the chest',
  'never scatters items on the ground',
  'halts instead of spinning when the chest fills up',
  'comes home to the start cell facing the way it started',
  'mines nothing on ground that is already flat',
];
---

<Base
  title="Unit 7: Autonomous Extraction at the Face — SipsCo"
  description="A SipsCo R&D field report on Unit 7: how the autonomous extraction unit levels a pad, how it knows when to stop, and what it structurally refuses to do."
>
  <Header />

  <main id="main" class="paper-main">
    <article class="paper">
      <header class="paper-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Field report &middot; Research &amp; Development</p>
        <h1>Unit 7</h1>
        <p class="paper-sub">Autonomous Extraction at the Face</p>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="s1">
        <h2 id="s1"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">1.</span> Summary</h2>
        <p class="paper-lead">
          Unit 7 levels a fifteen-by-fifteen pad to the elevation at which it
          is set down, and hauls what it removes to a chest at the edge of the
          work. It runs unattended. It has been cutting the north face since
          spring. This report covers what the unit does, what it refuses to
          do, and how we satisfied ourselves that the difference holds.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="s2">
        <h2 id="s2"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">2.</span> Layers, not columns</h2>
        <p>
          The obvious way to level ground is column by column: find the high
          point, chase it down, move on. Unit 7 does not do this. It sweeps
          the entire area at its starting elevation, rises one block, sweeps
          it again, and repeats until nothing remains overhead.
        </p>
        <p>
          This costs more walking. We accept the cost because a column scan
          walks <em>underneath</em> things &mdash; an overhang, a floating leaf
          canopy, anything whose support has already been removed. A column
          scan finds clear air above the column and calls it finished. The
          layer sweep does not have this failure mode, because it visits every
          cell at every elevation on the way up.
        </p>
        <p>
          Within a layer the unit walks a serpentine path &mdash; down one
          column, up the next &mdash; so that consecutive cells are always
          neighbours and no move is wasted returning to a row start. Each
          layer reverses the direction of the one below it, so the unit begins
          the next layer where it finished the last rather than trekking back
          to the corner.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="s3">
        <h2 id="s3"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">3.</span> Knowing when to stop</h2>
        <p>
          This is the interesting part of the problem, and the part that took
          the longest.
        </p>
        <p>
          An extraction unit can see exactly one block above itself. It cannot
          survey the site. It cannot know how tall the hill is. What it can do
          is notice, for free, whether the cell above the one it is standing
          in is occupied &mdash; and it stands in every cell of every layer.
          Finishing a layer therefore tells the unit whether
          the <em>next</em> layer has anything in it, which is how a tree
          keeps the unit climbing even when the leaf layers sit over open
          air.
        </p>
        <p>
          That lookahead reaches one block. Debris floating with a wider gap
          beneath it would be missed, so the first four layers are swept
          unconditionally before the unit is permitted to trust an empty
          result. A ceiling of thirty-two layers above the starting elevation
          bounds the job in the event that something above the pad is taller
          than anyone expected.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="s4">
        <h2 id="s4"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">4.</span> What the unit will not do</h2>
        <p>
          The guarantees below are structural. They are not settings, and an
          operator cannot switch them off by accident.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It will not cut below the floor.</strong> The elevation at
          which the unit is placed is the floor. The unit never aims a dig at
          or beneath it. A crew can set the floor by hand, walk away, and find
          it intact.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It will not leave the pad.</strong> Every leg of every route
          is axis-aligned and every destination lies inside the work area, so
          there is no sequence of moves that carries the unit outside the
          fifteen-by-fifteen. This includes the chest, which sits one block
          behind the near edge and has never been mined.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It will not scatter the haul.</strong> A dig with nowhere to
          put the block drops it on the ground, so the unit refuses to begin a
          cell with a full inventory &mdash; it returns and empties first. The
          delivery instruction is worse than it looks: told to deposit into
          nothing, it throws the material on the floor and reports success.
          The unit therefore confirms a real container is present before it
          hands anything over, and holds its load rather than dumping it into
          the dirt if one is not.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It will not burn its own fuel reserve.</strong> Coal,
          charcoal and blaze rods are recognised as fuel and are never
          delivered to the chest.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It will not spin.</strong> If the unit comes home full and
          leaves still full, it would bounce off the chest once per cell
          indefinitely. It stops instead, and says why.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It will not lose to gravel.</strong> Sand and gravel fall
          into the gap the instant it is cleared. Each block is re-cut up to
          eight times before the unit concedes the square.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="s5">
        <h2 id="s5"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">5.</span> Endurance</h2>
        <p>
          The unit refuels itself, topping up when its reserve falls below two
          hundred blocks of travel. It continuously computes the cost of
          returning home from wherever it currently stands, and keeps a margin
          of sixteen above that figure. When the margin is gone it comes home
          under its own power rather than stopping in the field.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the inventory fills, the unit walks to the chest, empties
          everything that is not fuel, and resumes at the exact cell it left.
          This accounts for essentially all of Unit 7's downtime. The 94%
          uptime figure quoted elsewhere is not a reliability measure; it is
          the fraction of the shift the unit is not standing at a chest,
          waiting for someone to come and take the dirt away.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="s6">
        <h2 id="s6"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">6.</span> The second unit</h2>
        <p>
          A companion unit patrols a ten-by-ten area collecting loose
          material. It cruises one block above the litter rather than walking
          through it, because a unit cannot recover items sharing its own
          position; clearing each cell downward also makes coverage
          independent of which way the unit happens to be facing. Under a low
          roof it drops to ground level and sweeps sideways instead &mdash;
          coverage is then partial, and it reports this rather than implying a
          clean pass. It runs a full circuit, sleeps an hour, and runs
          another.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="s7">
        <h2 id="s7"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">7.</span> Verification</h2>
        <p>
          Both units are exercised against a stubbed voxel world that
          implements the movement and inspection interface without requiring a
          live site. Thirteen cases run against it, and their names are the
          guarantees restated:
        </p>
        <ul class="test-names">
          {testNames.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>
          We would rather find out on a workbench than on the north face.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="s8">
        <h2 id="s8"><span class="sec-no" aria-hidden="true">8.</span> Availability</h2>
        <p>
          Unit 7's routines are not a trade secret. Both programs and the test
          harness are published in full. Run them on your own fleet &mdash; we
          ask only that you tell us what we got wrong.
        </p>
        <p class="paper-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >Read the source on GitHub<span class="visually-hidden"
              >&nbsp;(opens in a new tab)</span
            ></a
          >
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="appendix" aria-labelledby="appx">
        <h2 id="appx">Appendix A &mdash; Specifications</h2>
        <p>
          Figures below are the shipped defaults. Each is set at the head of
          the program named beneath it and may be changed by the operator; the
          identifier column gives the name it is set under.
        </p>

        {specs.map((s) => (
          <div class="spec">
            <h3 id={s.id}>{s.heading}</h3>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table aria-labelledby={s.id}>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Parameter</th>
                    <th scope="col">Value</th>
                    <th scope="col">Identifier</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {s.rows.map(([label, value, ident]) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{label}</th>
                      <td>{value}</td>
                      <td><code>{ident}</code></td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p class="spec-source">{s.source}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </section>

      <footer class="paper-foot">
        <p><a class="text-link" href="/rnd/">&larr; Back to Research &amp; Development</a></p>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .paper-main {
    padding: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem) 1.25rem clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5rem);
  }

  .paper {
    /* Narrower than the marketing pages: this is meant to be read start to
       finish, not scanned. */
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .paper-head {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
    padding-bottom: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .paper-head h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 6vw, 3.2rem);
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .paper-sub {
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 2.4vw, 1.3rem);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0;
  }

  .paper section { margin-bottom: 2.25rem; }

  .paper h2 {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .sec-no {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    margin-right: 0.4rem;
  }

  .paper p {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }

  .paper section p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* The abstract carries the weight of the whole document, so it is set a
     step up from the body. */
  .paper-lead {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .paper strong { font-weight: 650; }

  .test-names {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .test-names li { margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }

  .paper-cta { margin-top: 1.25rem; }

  /* Appendix: a datasheet, so it is set tighter and more mechanically than
     the running prose above it. */
  .appendix { margin-top: 2.75rem; }

  .spec { margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .spec h3 {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  /* Three columns do not fit a narrow phone; let the table scroll inside its
     own box rather than pushing the page sideways.

     The identifier column is the reason this appendix exists, and on a phone
     it starts off-screen, so the container carries the classic shadow hint:
     the two `local` gradients ride the content and cover the shadows when
     there is nothing more that way, leaving a shadow visible only on the
     edge you can actually scroll towards. */
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background:
      linear-gradient(to right, var(--surface), transparent) 0 0 / 28px 100% no-repeat local,
      linear-gradient(to left, var(--surface), transparent) 100% 0 / 28px 100% no-repeat local,
      radial-gradient(farthest-side at 0 50%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 20%, transparent), transparent) 0 0 / 14px 100% no-repeat scroll,
      radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 50%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 20%, transparent), transparent) 100% 0 / 14px 100% no-repeat scroll;
    background-color: var(--surface);
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    min-width: 26rem;
  }

  thead th {
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-weight: 600;
    background: var(--surface-alt);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  th, td {
    padding: 0.5rem 0.7rem;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  tbody th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 450;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  tbody tr + tr th,
  tbody tr + tr td { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }

  tbody td:nth-child(2) { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.92em;
    color: var(--accent);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The filename is a path, so it is set as one rather than as a label. */
  .spec-source {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-soft) 75%, transparent);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
  }

  .paper-foot {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .paper-foot p { margin: 0; }

  /* Two pages on paper, which is the whole conceit. Drop the site chrome,
     print on white, and spell out the one external URL since a printed link
     is otherwise a dead end. */
  @media print {
    .paper-main { padding: 0; }

    .paper {
      max-width: none;
      font-size: 9.6pt;
      line-height: 1.38;
    }

    .paper-head {
      margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
      padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .paper-head h1 { font-size: 19pt; }
    .paper-sub { font-size: 11pt; }
    .paper section { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
    .paper h2 { font-size: 10.6pt; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
    .paper p { margin-bottom: 0.42rem; }
    .paper-lead { font-size: 10pt; }
    .test-names { margin-bottom: 0.42rem; }
    .test-names li { margin-bottom: 0.1rem; }
    .paper-cta { margin-top: 0.6rem; }

    /* Never strand a heading at the foot of a page. Sections deliberately do
       NOT get break-inside: avoid — section 4 is long enough that refusing to
       split it pushes the whole block to a fresh page and wastes the
       remainder, which is what put this over two pages. */
    .paper h2 { break-after: avoid; }

    .paper-cta .btn {
      border: 1px solid #000;
      background: none !important;
      color: #000 !important;
      padding: 0.3rem 0.6rem;
    }

    .paper-cta a::after {
      content: " — " attr(href);
      font-size: 9pt;
    }

    .paper-foot { display: none; }

    /* The report proper is two pages. An appendix belongs on its own sheet
       rather than eating into them, so it starts a new page and each table
       is kept whole. */
    .appendix {
      break-before: page;
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .spec { margin-top: 0.9rem; break-inside: avoid; }
    .spec h3 { font-size: 10pt; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }

    /* Nothing scrolls on paper, so the hint gradients only muddy the print. */
    .table-scroll {
      overflow-x: visible;
      border-color: #999;
      background: none;
    }

    table { font-size: 8.6pt; min-width: 0; }
    th, td { padding: 0.28rem 0.45rem; }
    thead th { background: none; border-bottom: 1px solid #000; }
    code { color: #000; }
  }
</style>
